Bluetooth: leave LE address resolution to the host on Unisoc Marlin3

The kernel turns the controller's address resolution on whenever the
controller reports LL Privacy: hci_update_accept_list_sync() enables it each
time it programs the accept list for a passive scan. An L2CAP LE connect made
while not advertising goes through such a scan (hci_connect_le_scan()): the
kernel puts the peer's address in the accept list and connects when it sees
the peer advertise.

On the F50, with an Android head unit as the peer (it advertises with a
resolvable private address), the Marlin3 firmware (manufacturer 0x01ec)
reported that peer's advertising as the public identity address
00:00:00:00:00:00 once address resolution was on, while the host had
programmed no IRK at all (the resolving list was empty) and other devices
advertising with private addresses were reported as they were. The accept
list holds the peer's private address, so none of its reports passed the
filter, and every connect timed out after about 40 s. The state outlived
HCI_Reset and LE Clear Resolving List; a power cycle of the device cleared
it, and it came back after later connections to the same peer. How the
firmware comes to match that peer is not known.

Measured with raw HCI commands, 5 s passive scans for that peer:
  address resolution on,  accept list: none of its reports
  address resolution off, accept list: 42 reports
  address resolution on,  no filter:   its reports as 00:00:00:00:00:00
With address resolution turned off during the connect's scan, the
connection was made within a second.

Clear LL Privacy from the LE features of these controllers, so that the
kernel never enables address resolution in them and resolves private
addresses itself.

--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
@@ -1239,6 +1239,16 @@
 		return rp->status;
 
 	memcpy(hdev->le_features, rp->features, 8);
+
+	/* With address resolution on, the Unisoc (Spreadtrum) Marlin3 firmware
+	 * can report a peer's resolvable private addresses as the identity
+	 * 00:00:00:00:00:00 although the host gave it no IRK, and keeps doing so
+	 * across HCI_Reset. An accept list entry for that peer's address then
+	 * never matches and a connection to it never starts. Leave address
+	 * resolution to the host.
+	 */
+	if (hdev->manufacturer == 0x01ec)
+		hdev->le_features[0] &= ~HCI_LE_LL_PRIVACY;
 
 	return rp->status;
 }
